Unreleased
---
Feature
- QxToast is reported as a Notification and QxSplashScreen names its message. A
  toast is a transient notification, but a plain QWidget as far as the default
  accessibility is concerned, so it was announced as a Client - or not at all,
  since it is not focusable; a QAccessible factory, installed by the QxToast
  constructor because a toast can be shown on its own, now reports the
  Notification role. The splash draws its message into the pixmap instead of
  placing it in a child widget, so the message is mirrored into the accessible
  name. tst_AppAccessible

- QxAppShell::addLayoutGroup() adds the layout group every ribbon shell carries:
  a Save and a Restore action, both wired to saveLayout()/restoreLayout() and
  both reporting through the status bar. A shell persists its layout whether or
  not anyone can ask it to, and the two demo shells had been writing the same
  page->addGroup(tr("Layout")) block out by hand - the group is now one call.
  tst_QxAppShell::layoutGroup
- QxAppShell::createStandardShell() builds the standard shell in one call and
  returns its ribbon page: the page carrying the group above, the status bar
  showing "Ready", and the layout stored when the window closes. The three
  pieces a shell is made of - the ribbon, the docks, the window - were already
  wired to each other; what was missing was the scaffolding the layout group
  needs a page for, which every application was assembling in the same order.
  tst_QxAppShell::standardShell

- qxapp offers the bundled theme registration as a one-liner. The four families -
  soft, classic, flat and slate - each in its light and dark variant, were
  registered by hand in every demo that ships res/themes.qrc, each with its own
  private file reader; qxthemebundle.h now provides readBundledTheme() (the file
  reader) and registerBundledThemes(engine) (the eight themes in one call), which
  AppShellDemo and Example_RibbonStyle use. A theme whose .css is missing is
  skipped rather than throwing, so a partial resource tree is safe

- registerBundledThemes() ships a fifth theme family, accent. The other four mark
  the selected tab with a rule, a lifted card or a changed label; accent is the
  first to fill the tab with the accent colour itself - a solid block with a white
  label, the unselected tabs left transparent in grey and a hovered tab washed in
  the palest accent. Like the rest it is generated by tools/themes/build_themes.py
  from chrome_accent.css and the palette in that script, so the two files,
  accent_light.css and accent_dark.css, follow the same source - never edit them
  directly.

Interface
- Rename QxNavBar to QxNavTabBar. The class is a QxTabBar-derived tab navigation
  bar with a show/hide-tabs customize menu, but its name sat one letter from
  QxNavigationBar - a QWidget, the QxAppShell vertical rail, sharing neither a
  base nor a purpose with it. The two read as variants of one another while being
  unrelated, so the tab bar is renamed and the rail keeps its name. Breaking, and
  it lands in this minor; no forward-compatibility alias is kept, because the
  point of the rename is to stop the two names from looking like siblings.
  See doc/pages/migration.md.

0.1.2
---
Bugfix
- RibbonAppWindow::updateWindowFlags() left the ribbon laid out for the next
  event loop pass, so the window button group it had just resized stayed on the
  previous boundary for one frame - which is the "button group overlap for a
  moment" doc/pages/limitations.md records. The deferral comes from
  RibbonBar::resizeRibbon(), and this is the one path where it costs something
  visible: the base implementation re-shows the window, so the frame is on screen
  before the re-layout happens. It now asks for the layout with
  RibbonBar::resizeRibbonNow() and the groups are placed by the time the call
  returns. tst_RibbonAppWindow::flagsChangeLaysTheGroupsOutImmediately
- QxNavigationBar did not stretch its entries across the rail. The bar pins
  itself to QSizePolicy::Fixed horizontally, so the shell's layout gives it
  the width of its widest entry, but the entries themselves were plain
  QToolButtons with the default fixed width - a shorter entry therefore
  floated in the middle of the rail instead of spanning it, which is what
  the AppShell demo showed with three entries hinting 128, 104 and 68 pixels
  inside a 132 pixel rail. The entries now expand horizontally, so each one
  takes the rail's full inner width while the rail keeps its own width.
  tst_QxNavigationBar covers it.

0.1.1
---
Cleanup
- Remove the Qt version branches below 5.15, which K1 set as the minimum: the
  QActionGroup::setExclusive fallback, the missing m_togglable initialiser
  (already defaulted to true in the member initialiser list), the togglable
  no-op, and the QFontMetrics::width fallback in QxToolButton

Bugfix
- QX_APP_DEPRECATED_SINCE(major, minor) never answered true for a deprecation
  that had already happened: QX_APP_DISABLE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE defaulted to
  QX_APP_VERSION, so the deprecation point was compared against the version being
  built - (0,1,0) > (0,1,0) is false - and the answer flipped back to false on
  every minor bump. The default is now a fixed floor, so an API stays declared for
  as long as it is inside its removal buffer (K26), and raising the cutoff is what
  compiles it out. tst_qxdeprecated covers all seven libraries
